course: dictionary
lesson: 15971
title: Unwise choices
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word unwise is made of two parts. Un- means not, and wise means sensible. So unwise means not sensible, and likely to cause trouble.
explain: Look at the words around it. «It would be unwise to swim after a big meal.» The clue is cause trouble: swimming straight after eating can make you feel ill, so the choice is not sensible.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «It would be not sensible to swim after a big meal.» It still makes sense, so unwise means not sensible here.
fact: Unwise is an adjective: unwise, more unwise, most unwise.
fact: The prefix un- means not, so wise becomes unwise, which means not sensible.
fact: Useful phrases: it is unwise to, an unwise choice, an unwise decision.
fact: «It would be unwise to swim after a big meal.» means swimming then could cause trouble.
fact: Wise and unwise are opposites: a wise choice is sensible, and an unwise choice can lead to trouble.
exercise: «It would be unwise to cross the road without looking.» What does unwise mean? | Very clever and careful | Quick and easy | *Not sensible, and likely to cause trouble | Happy and calm
exercise: Sam says, «Sharing my password with a stranger is an unwise choice.» Why? | It is a fun game to play | It makes the stranger happy | It is always the best idea | *It could lead to trouble, so it is not sensible
exercise: What does the un- in unwise mean? | *Not | Again | Before | Very
test: «Lending your house key to everyone you meet would be an unwise decision.» What does unwise mean here? | Quick to do | *Likely to cause trouble | Kind and helpful | Easy to find
test: Which phrase means the same as unwise? | Very wise | Very quick | Very quiet | *Not sensible
exam: Pick the word that fits: «Going out alone in the dark late at night is an ___ choice.» | wisely | wise | *unwise | wiser
